    "article": "A small hotel has installed smart controls to reduce energy use. Sensors have been attached to doors and windows, and room temperatures are checked in real time. When a guest leaves, the heating can be lowered automatically. At night, lights in empty meeting rooms are switched off. The system has been tested for three months, and energy use has already fallen by twelve percent.\n\nHowever, the manager knows that smart equipment is not a complete solution. The hotel's electricity is partly produced from renewable energy, whose supply is not always steady. Solar power is available when the sun shines, but demand may be highest at another time. For this reason, a battery will be installed next month so that extra power can be stored and used later. Staff must also be trained to operate the controls correctly. The manager expects the technology to improve efficiency, but she says simple actions—closing windows and turning off unused equipment—should not be forgotten.",
