    "article": "During a weekly review, Nora noticed that the team was entering the same customer data twice. She did not want to tell her coworkers that they were doing their jobs badly, so she prepared a specific suggestion. She said, “I recommend that we use one shared form, and I suggest that the supervisor review it before Friday.” Her wording focused on the process rather than blaming a person.\n\nThe supervisor asked what benefit the change would bring. Nora explained that a shared form could reduce errors and help new employees find information faster. Another coworker suggested testing the form with one client first. The team agreed to do that and asked Nora to have the digital form prepared by Wednesday. Nora was supposed to send instructions as well, so she got a colleague to check them for clarity. By putting her idea forward gently and answering questions with evidence, she avoided getting a reputation for interfering. Her suggestion became a small trial rather than an immediate rule.",
