    "article": "Small talk is a short, friendly conversation about an ordinary topic. At work, it can break the ice when people are introduced for the first time or are waiting for a meeting to begin. Common safe topics include the weather, the journey to work, or weekend plans. The purpose is usually to make an interaction feel less awkward, not to exchange important business information.\n\nDifferent questions need different kinds of answers. Where did you have lunch? needs a place, while When does the session start? needs a time. A request such as Could you send me the file? needs an action response, for example, Sure, I'll do it after lunch. It does not need a yes/no fact about the file. In natural conversation, an answer may be a short fragment: Down the hall, Not yet, or In a minute. These are acceptable because they answer the function of the question. Repeating one word from the question without answering it is not enough.",
    "listening": "1. Where is the visitor badge? A On the front desk. B At ten o'clock. C Yes, I visited.\n2. Has Mina sent the revised schedule yet? A The schedule is blue. B She's checking it now. C In Room 4.\n3. Would you introduce me to the new supplier? A Sure, he's beside the window. B Yesterday morning. C The supplier has a website.\n4. Why don't we meet after lunch? A That's a good idea. B On the lunch menu. C I met her once.",
    "grammarNote": "• who 人、where 地、when 時、why 原因、how 方法、which 選擇；Could/Would you...? 常是請求。\n• 常錯：只回 yes/no，卻不處理動作。辨認：Where is Ken? A At reception B At three。無提示：替 Could you resend it? 作自然間接回應。遷移：把四種問句放入飯店情境。",
