    "goal": "先分 idea、test、evidence、decision。could 是提案；will review...before deciding 才是流程。",
    "article": "The customer support team wanted to reduce the number of repeated questions about delivery. At first, each employee came up with ideas alone. One person suggested a longer help page, while another proposed an automatic message. During the meeting, the team wrote every idea on a board without judging it immediately. This brainstorming stage helped quieter employees put ideas forward.\n\nNext, the group looked for connections. Building on the automatic-message idea, Hana suggested sending customers a short update only when an order changed status. Leo bounced the idea off a warehouse coworker, who warned that some status data arrived late. The team therefore added a manual review for messages that had not been updated in twelve hours. They also decided not to send extra messages when nothing had changed.\n\nRather than implement the system for every customer, the team tested it with fifty orders. The trial showed that delivery questions fell, but one phrase—Your item is moving—was unclear. Customers thought it meant the package would arrive that day. The team replaced it with Your item has left the warehouse; the estimated delivery date is unchanged. The idea improved because the group combined creativity, operational evidence, and customer feedback. Thinking outside the box started the process, but careful testing made the solution usable.\n\nFor the next test, the team will compare the revised message with no message at all. It will measure not only the number of questions but also whether customers correctly understand the delivery stage.",
    "listening": "Mina: We need ideas for reducing waiting at the front desk.\nOwen: What if guests completed the basic form before arriving?\nSara: Building on that, we could send the form with the reservation confirmation.\nMina: Good, but let's test it with weekend guests first. Some may need help completing it.\nOwen: I'll ask reception staff to record how many guests arrive with a finished form.\nSara: And I'll prepare a two-question survey about whether the instructions were clear.\nMina: Great. We'll review both sets of data on Monday before deciding whether to implement it.\n\nOwen: Should the message go to every weekend guest?\n\nMina: Only to people whose reservation is confirmed before Friday noon. A guest who books at the front desk has already arrived, so an advance form would not reduce waiting.\n\nSara: What information can we request? The old form asks for passport details.\n\nMina: Remove that section from the test. We only need the guest's name, arrival time, and whether accessibility support is required. Sensitive identification will still be checked in person.\n\nOwen: I'll brief the reception team and create a separate code for guests who received the form but could not open it.\n\nSara: That will help us distinguish unclear instructions from a technical problem.\n\nMina: Exactly. Bring the completion rate, average waiting time, and survey comments on Monday. One number alone will not tell us whether the idea works.",
    "grammarNote": "• 先分 idea、test、evidence、decision。could 是提案；will review...before deciding 才是流程。\n• 常錯：把 Sara 的提議當已全面實施。辨認：What will happen before implementation? 無提示：用 suggest that 寫測試要求。遷移：為 BOM 系統提出一個想法、風險、測試指標。",
