    "grammar": "G24 used to 三組",
    "goal": "used to + V 過去曾；be used to + N/V-ing 已習慣；get used to + N/V-ing 逐漸習慣。",
    "article": "When Arun moved to Taipei for a six-month project, he expected language to be his biggest challenge. Instead, his daily food routine created the first culture shock. He used to eat a warm, savoury breakfast at home, but many nearby shops offered sweet bread or cold sandwiches. At first, he bought the same item every morning because ordering something unfamiliar felt difficult.\n\nA coworker invited him to a traditional breakfast shop and explained the menu one section at a time. Arun tried warm soy milk, a rice roll, and a pancake with egg. He did not enjoy every texture immediately. One drink was an acquired taste: the flavour seemed unusual on the first visit, but became pleasant after several tries. Within a month, Arun was used to ordering in the busy shop and had learned which dishes were common staple foods.\n\nAdapting did not mean giving up food from home. On Fridays, Arun cooked for his coworkers. The smell of his family's spice mixture reminded him of home, while the shared meal allowed his coworkers to ask questions about his food culture. By the end of the project, Arun had not replaced one diet with another. He had added new choices and become more comfortable with difference. The experience also changed his view of culture shock: it can be uncomfortable, but it can begin a useful learning process when people are willing to explain and try.",
    "listening": "Host: You used to live in Seoul. What changed when you moved here?\nGuest: I used to eat dinner quite late, but my new coworkers usually eat before seven. At first, I wasn't used to leaving the office for dinner so early.\nHost: Has that become easier?\nGuest: Yes. I'm used to it now, and I enjoy sharing a meal with the team. I still cook Korean food on weekends because it reminds me of home.\nHost: What advice would you give a newcomer?\nGuest: Try a small amount, ask what is in the dish, and don't expect yourself to like everything immediately.\n\nHost: Was there a food that took longer to enjoy?\n\nGuest: Fermented bean paste was an acquired taste for me. The smell was unfamiliar, so at first I avoided it. A coworker served a small amount with rice and explained that it was a staple food in her family. That context helped.\n\nHost: Did you stop eating food from home?\n\nGuest: Not at all. Adapting doesn't mean replacing one food culture with another. I now cook a Korean soup for our shared lunch once a month, and coworkers bring dishes from their own homes. We check allergies in advance and label ingredients.\n\nHost: What will you bring next time?\n\nGuest: A mild soup. I'll send the ingredient list on Monday so everyone can make an informed choice before Thursday's meal.",
    "grammarNote": "• used to + V 過去曾；be used to + N/V-ing 已習慣；get used to + N/V-ing 逐漸習慣。\n• 常錯：I am used to eat early。辨認：I ___ eating early now. A used to B am used to。無提示：She is getting used to ___. 遷移：比較澳洲／台灣一個過去習慣與現在適應。",
