    "article": "Document A — A host's listing\n\nMina has a spare room near a railway station. It was empty most weeks, so she listed it on a home-sharing platform as a source of income on the side. Her listing says the apartment can hold two guests and that the kitchen is shared. The nightly price includes cleaning, but it does not include the city's visitor charge. Guests must pay that amount through the platform before arrival.\n\nMina likes the system because she can make use of space that would otherwise remain empty. During a festival weekend, nearby hotels were full to capacity, and her room was booked within an hour. However, she does not accept every request. She checks a guest's verified record and sends the building rules before confirming a reservation. Quiet hours begin at ten, and visitors who are not included in the booking are not allowed.\n\nDocument B — A guest's message to customer service\n\nI booked Mina's room because the first screen showed a lower total than a hotel. At checkout, a visitor charge and a platform fee were added. The final amount was still reasonable, but I felt that the price had not been clear enough. I also paid a deposit. The host returned it after checking the room, yet the money was unavailable to me for five days, which left me temporarily out of pocket.\n\nThe room itself was clean, and Mina explained the building's rules and regulations before I arrived. My main complaint concerns price display, not the host. Please show every compulsory charge earlier. A thriving sharing business depends on trust from both sides: hosts need protection for their property, while guests need accurate costs and a clear process when something goes wrong.\n\nCustomer service replied that it would test a full-price display. It would also explain how long deposits may be held. The company did not promise to remove the charges; it promised to make them easier to see.",
    "listening": "Host: Hello, I received your request for Friday and Saturday. Before I confirm it, I'd like to check two details. The room is for one registered guest, and the building has quiet hours after ten. Is that suitable?\n\nGuest: Yes. I will be travelling alone. I may meet a colleague for dinner, but she won't come back to the apartment.\n\nHost: That's fine. The price shown on the final payment page includes cleaning and the platform fee. The city visitor charge is collected separately tomorrow. It is six pounds in total.\n\nGuest: Thanks for telling me. I saw a lower figure on the search page and wanted to make sure there would not be another large charge.\n\nHost: I understand. There is also a refundable deposit. The platform holds it; I don't receive the money. If no damage is reported, the release normally begins within twenty-four hours after checkout, although your bank may take longer to display it.\n\nGuest: All right. Could you also tell me the easiest way to get there from the airport?\n\nHost: Take the airport bus to Central Station. The apartment is a five-minute walk from there. I'll send a map and the entry code on Thursday afternoon. Please do not share the code with anyone.\n\nGuest: Perfect. I'll complete the payment now and look for your message on Thursday.",
    "grammarNote": "• 實際意思：雙篇題先分開記「誰說什麼」，再找兩篇相符或互補的證據。\n• 原句：Listing 說費用在付款前收；guest 說第一個畫面未完整顯示。兩者不矛盾：它們談不同畫面。\n• 結構：文件 A 的規則／文件 B 的經驗 → 可支持的最小結論。\n• 常錯：把 first screen 和 final payment page 當同一頁；把 promised clearer display 誤讀為 promised removal。\n• 辨認題：Which page has the complete price?（final payment page）無提示題：寫出客訴範圍。遷移題：比較訂位確認信與退款信，判斷哪個承諾已完成。",
