    "article": "Green Basket trial: report and data note\n\nA supermarket tested a carbon labelling scheme on three kinds of lunch boxes. Each label showed an estimated carbon footprint, from production to delivery, using the same calculation method. A green circle marked the lowest-emission choice within each product group. The purpose was not to tell customers what to buy. It was to give them accurate information so that they could make an informed choice.\n\nDuring the first week, only 18 per cent of interviewed shoppers noticed the label. In week two, the store moved it beside the price and added a short sign explaining that a smaller number meant fewer estimated emissions. Awareness rose to 46 per cent. Sales of the lowest-carbon lunch box increased from 120 to 150 units, while its price stayed at NT$95.\n\nThe result looked encouraging, but the manager warned against a simple conclusion. The product with the smallest carbon footprint was also the least expensive. Customers may have chosen it because it was the green choice, because it was cheaper, or for both reasons. In addition, an estimate is only comparable when products use the same measurement rules. A supplier using a different method might produce a number that looks lower without actually being more environmentally responsible.\n\nCustomer comments revealed another issue. Several shoppers thought the circle meant that the package could be recycled, although it actually referred to emissions. The store therefore plans to add separate symbols for carbon and packaging. It will also publish a short explanation of its calculation method. Carbon labels may catch on only if they are visible, easy to understand, and supported by trustworthy data.\n\nThe next trial will keep prices equal for one week. This change will help the team separate the possible effect of price from the effect of environmental information. Even then, the report will describe an association, not claim that one label alone changed every customer's behaviour.",
    "listening": "Presenter: Welcome to the second stage of our Green Basket trial. Last month, customers told us that the carbon figures were difficult to interpret, so we have redesigned the shelf labels.\n\nStore analyst: Each label now has two separate areas. The blue area shows whether the packaging can be recycled locally. The green area shows the product's estimated carbon footprint. Please do not describe a product as “zero carbon”; none of the items in this trial has that rating.\n\nDepartment manager: Are the figures directly comparable across all four suppliers?\n\nAnalyst: Three suppliers used our standard method. The fourth submitted a number from its own calculator, so we will display its data with a note until it can be recalculated. It should not receive the green-choice mark yet.\n\nPresenter: For the next seven days, two soup products will have the same promotional price. Staff should record which one is selected and ask a small sample of willing customers what information they noticed. Do not suggest an answer. We need to know whether they mention price, carbon, packaging, taste, or something else.\n\nManager: When will we review the results?\n\nPresenter: On Tuesday morning. Bring the sales totals and the completed customer forms. We will not decide on a permanent labelling scheme until the data have been checked.",
