    "article": "Internal note: language for the new service chatbot\n\nOur customer-service chatbot can produce human-like responses, remember details within one conversation, and suggest the next step. During testing, several staff members said that it seemed to have a mind of its own. That phrase is natural in everyday conversation, but it could create a misleading assumption in customer materials. The system processes information and predicts suitable words; our test has not shown that it has feelings or artificial consciousness.\n\nThis distinction matters when the bot makes a mistake. If a reply sounds confident, customers may believe that it is supported by evidence. In fact, the system can combine an old policy with a new product name and produce an answer that reads smoothly but is wrong. A human-like tone is not proof of human-like understanding. Just because a machine can describe disappointment, it does not mean that the machine experiences disappointment.\n\nThe review team found a second issue: technical bias. Here, bias refers to a repeated tendency in output, not to a single random error. When customers wrote very short messages, the bot often assumed that they wanted to cancel an order. Longer messages about late delivery were handled more accurately. Engineers are checking whether the training examples included too many short cancellation requests.\n\nFor the public launch, the bot may explain standard procedures and collect basic order details. It is not capable of approving exceptional refunds. When confidence is low or a request involves safety, legal responsibility, or a large payment, the conversation must be passed to a person. The screen will say, “I am an automated assistant,” and each answer will link to the relevant policy.\n\nThese steps do not settle the philosophical question of whether a future AI could be conscious. They solve a practical communication problem now: customers should know what the system can do, what evidence supports an answer, and when a human will take over.",
    "listening": "Product manager: Before tomorrow's trial, let's review the chatbot's answer about damaged batteries.\n\nSafety officer: The answer is polite, but it tells the customer to mail the battery back. That conflicts with our safety policy. A damaged battery must not be placed in ordinary post.\n\nEngineer: The bot copied part of an old return instruction. Its confidence score was high because the wording matched many previous requests, but those requests involved clothing, not batteries.\n\nManager: So a high confidence score does not guarantee a safe answer.\n\nEngineer: Correct. It measures how strongly the system matches a pattern; it does not check every real-world consequence. I can add a rule that sends any message containing “swollen battery”, “smoke”, or “overheating” directly to a specialist.\n\nSafety officer: Please include less obvious wording too. A customer may say that the phone feels unusually hot without using the word “overheating”.\n\nManager: Good point. For tomorrow, let's pause automatic return advice for all battery messages. The bot may collect the product model and contact number, then say that a safety specialist will call within thirty minutes.\n\nEngineer: I'll make that change and run twenty test messages tonight.\n\nSafety officer: Send me the results. I will check not only whether the trigger works, but also whether the wording clearly tells customers not to post the item.",
    "grammarNote": "• 實際意思：遇到 anthropomorphise 等陌生字，先看後文是否有定義、對比或例子，不必先查每個字。\n• 原句概念：people give a machine human qualities → anthropomorphise。\n• 結構：陌生字附近的 refers to、not...but...、冒號、例子是線索。\n• 常錯：只看字首亂猜；把作者轉述的說法當作者結論。\n• 辨認：sentient 旁若出現 able to feel，可推為「有感受能力」。無提示：寫出 bias 在本文的定義。遷移：從退貨政策找 exceptional 的範圍。",
