    "article": "Training experiment: one long review or four short reviews?\n\nA service company introduced a new return system and trained forty staff members. Everyone watched the same demonstration and completed the same ten practice cases. The staff were then divided into two equal groups. Group A reviewed the instructions for forty minutes on Friday. Group B reviewed for ten minutes on Friday, Monday, Wednesday, and the following Monday. The total review time was the same.\n\nImmediately after the first session, Group A answered 88 per cent of the questions correctly, compared with Group B's 81 per cent. A manager concluded that one long review was more effective. However, the training team had not yet measured long-term memory. When both groups took an unannounced test two weeks later, Group A scored 62 per cent and Group B scored 79 per cent. Group B also retrieved the correct exception rule more quickly in a timed customer case.\n\nThe result is consistent with spaced practice: information is more likely to stick when learners return to it after some forgetting has begun. That small mental effort helps them retrieve the idea instead of simply recognising a page they have just read. Learners can also make an association by connecting a rule with a realistic customer situation. This gives the memory more than one route for later retrieval.\n\nThe study has limits. It involved only forty people in one company, and Group B met the material on four separate days. Their result could partly reflect greater attention, not spacing alone. The data do not show that long sessions are always useless. A longer session may still be necessary when staff first learn a complex procedure. The safer conclusion is that short follow-up reviews improved later performance in this setting.\n\nThe company will now keep the first demonstration but replace the single Friday review with three brief retrieval questions over the next two weeks. Staff will answer before seeing the explanation. This design checks what they can retrieve and prevents an immediate rereading from creating a false feeling of mastery.",
    "listening": "Trainer: Before we start today's software practice, please close the instruction page. You will see three customer cases, one at a time. Choose an action first; then the explanation will appear.\n\nEmployee: I studied the guide for an hour yesterday. Wouldn't it be faster to keep it open?\n\nTrainer: It would make today's task easier, but our aim is to find out what you can retrieve without support. If you only recognise the answer while looking at the guide, you may feel ready even though the rule has not entered long-term memory.\n\nEmployee: What happens if I choose the wrong action?\n\nTrainer: Nothing is recorded in your performance review. Note the reason for the error and try a similar case on Thursday. The second case will use a different product and customer, so you cannot succeed by memorising the exact sentence.\n\nEmployee: Will we repeat all twenty rules every time?\n\nTrainer: No. The system gives more practice to rules you miss and less to rules you retrieve correctly several times. It also mixes old material with one new procedure. That makes the practice less predictable, but it provides better evidence of what you actually remember.\n\nEmployee: So I should answer before opening the guide.\n\nTrainer: Exactly. After all three cases, you may review the full explanation and make an association with a real call you have handled.",
    "grammarNote": "• 實際意思：最佳答案要保留研究的對象、時間與限制。\n• 原句：improved later performance in this setting，不是 spacing always beats long study。\n• 結構：result + limitation + qualified conclusion。\n• 常錯：選項偷換 immediate/later、recognise/retrieve、association/cause、forty/all workers。\n• 辨認：哪一項是實測？two-week scores。無提示：寫出一個研究限制。遷移：閱讀小型 A/B 測試後排除含 always、everyone、proved 的選項。",
