    "article": "Speaking desk at the International Service Fair\n\nLina works in purchasing and can read English emails accurately, but she feels nervous when an overseas supplier calls. She worries that a pause will make her sound unprepared. At the International Service Fair, her manager asks her to staff the information desk for one hour. Lina's first reaction is to avoid the situation. Instead, they design three practical steps that move her slightly outside her comfort zone without giving her an impossible task.\n\nFirst, Lina prepares four sentence frames: asking a visitor to repeat, checking a number, explaining that she will confirm information, and passing a technical question to a specialist. She does not memorise a complete speech. A fixed script could fail if a visitor asks something unexpected, while flexible frames can support several conversations.\n\nSecond, the manager changes the success measure. Lina does not have to sound like a native speaker in order to do the job well. A typical speaker pauses, corrects a word, or asks for clarification. Her goal is to identify the visitor's request and give a reliable next step. If she communicates the wrong collection time confidently, perfect pronunciation will not make the answer useful.\n\nThird, Lina completes the hour in two stages. For the first fifteen minutes, a colleague stands nearby but speaks only if Lina asks for help. For the remaining time, the colleague moves to another desk. Lina records each request with a short code, so she can see evidence of completed tasks instead of judging the session from one awkward moment.\n\nAt the end, Lina has handled nine enquiries. She asked two people to repeat dates and transferred one customs question. No visitor complained. She still felt self-conscious during the first conversation, and the exercise did not remove all speaking anxiety. It did show that she could act while nervous, make small corrections, and finish a real workplace task. Her next step is a ten-minute supplier call with the order details prepared beside her.",
    "listening": "Supervisor: Before the fair opens, let's practise what to do when you don't understand a visitor. What is your first step?\n\nLina: I can say, “Could you repeat the date more slowly, please?” Then I repeat the number back to check it.\n\nSupervisor: Good. If the question is about today's collection times, the blue sheet has the confirmed schedule. If it concerns next month's service, don't guess. Tell the visitor you will check with the transport team and ask for an email address.\n\nLina: What if I know the answer but forget one English word?\n\nSupervisor: Describe the meaning or point to the correct item on the public map. A short pause is fine. Focus on completing the task rather than hiding every mistake.\n\nLina: And if a visitor becomes impatient?\n\nSupervisor: Keep your voice calm and state the action you are taking. You can say, “I want to give you accurate information, so I'm checking this now.” If the person still cannot wait, offer the service desk's phone number. Do not promise an immediate call unless a staff member has accepted the handover.\n\nLina: After each conversation, should I write a full report?\n\nSupervisor: No. Mark the request type and whether it was answered, checked, or transferred. We will review the pattern after thirty minutes, not interrupt you after every minor error.\n\nLina: All right. I'll start with the prepared sentence frames and ask for support when the information is uncertain.",
